WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
We will make most conversations
unnecessary. Slack's mission is to make

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.960
work life simpler,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.620
more pleasant, and more productive. Our
original promise was to kill email,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.760
but we ended up creating

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.300
a different type of problem. Endless
channel noise, ping culture, and

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.920
constant back and

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.940
forth threads that talk about work instead
of doing it. Slack was built on

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.280
three assumptions about

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.810
the person using it, but a lot has
changed. The first assumption was that

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.040
people are doing the work.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.880
Today, handing off work to AI is normal.
Agents can finish a task on their

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.160
own inside the tools

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.210
your team already use. The second
assumption was your team is online when

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.400
you are. Now work happens

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.030
when you're offline. Agents run 24/7. Team
mates are globally distributed

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.480
and keep different hours.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.530
And what's waiting for you when you come
back is a pile of messages and

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.160
channel notifications

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.840
that you need to sort, sift, and orientate
yourself around before you can

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:03.440
decide if your

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.980
response is needed. The third assumption
was that you belong to one team

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.040
with one job.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.940
Now people are working beyond their usual
roles. A designer can analyze

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.240
customer data.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.930
An engineer can draft launch copy. A
marketer can build a prototype. So

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:21.840
what if Slack didn't

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.370
just help us talk about work? What if
Slack could take a conversation,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.600
figure out what needs to

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.160
happen next, and get it started? There are
three elements to make this work.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.320
A twin that represents

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.560
you, a producer that turns discussion into
work, and Reins, where you

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.440
decide what an AI can do on

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.540
its own and what needs your approval.
Let's see what that looks like for

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.600
Janice. She's a product

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.190
lead at Parcelo, helping customers find
contact information for their

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:53.520
sales leads. People across

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.450
the company turn to her for answers,
updates, and decisions. She knows the

00:01:58.450 --> 00:01:59.840
details and remembers

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.920
why decisions were made, but keeping
everyone moving takes time away from

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.200
her high leverage work.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.850
But Janice has a secret. She has a twin.
So let's see what's waiting for

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.040
her on Monday morning.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.080
So it's Monday morning, and her twin has
already handled 30 questions while

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.920
she was away. But one

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.700
still needs her judgment. Janice is
launching a feature that automatically

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.200
finds contact information

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.790
for sales leads. Over the weekend, a pilot
customer uploaded 5,000 leads,

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:31.920
but only had enough credits

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.100
for 2,000. Engineering is asking how to
handle this use case. Her twin

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.720
brings her the options and

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.460
what they mean for Thursday's launch.
Since Thursday is only three days

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.000
away, she wants to have a call

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.730
with Jonas to work through the details of
the customer experience. The

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:48.720
first topic they work

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.270
through is design. The design agent drafts
mockups as they talk, while the

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:54.320
producer turns their

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.150
conversation into Jira tickets, test
cases, and support drafts. Before they

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.560
settle on the solution,

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.910
they both want to understand what Maya
heard from the pilot customer. She

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:05.520
isn't on the call,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.220
so the producer asks Maya's twin to bring
the feedback they need to make

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.560
the right decision.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.440
Janice and Jonas focus on getting the
experience right. The agents handle

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.440
the work around them.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.570
Janice approves the solution, and the work
prepared during the call is

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.040
saved and sent to the system of

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.250
record. She doesn't leave the conversation
with the list of follow-up tasks.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.400
She just confirms the

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.930
decision, and the work is already
prepared. On Saturday, Devin and Sales

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.240
ask whether Thursday's

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.240
launch was still on track. Her twin
answered with what it knew, while

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:37.040
making it clear that

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.030
Janice still had a decision to make. Now
that she's confirmed, it goes back

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.880
to Devin with the update,

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.420
without Janice having to remember who was
waiting. But why would Janice let

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.040
something speak for her?

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.400
Reins is how Janice stays in control. She
can see what her twin has done,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.940
correct it, and decide what it can handle
without her. As she builds

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.800
confidence in its work,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.440
she can give it more responsibility. Some
decisions still need her approval,

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.800
and company rules still apply. But she
doesn't have to trust it with

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.400
everything to get the most

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.170
value from it. People are getting used to
asking AI before asking a

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.800
colleague. With the twin,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.890
they can get answers from Janice's
knowledge without interrupting her. We're

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:27.680
also moving away

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.360
from giving AI individual tasks to
trusting it with more autonomous work.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.240
Janice makes a decision,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.720
the agent turns it into specs, tickets,
and whatever else the team needs next.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.480
Retrieval helps agents find the relevant
context, tool use lets them act on it,

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.480
and agent to agent communication lets them
get answers from another twin.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.700
Slack supplies the reasoning behind that
work, what was decided, why, and

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:55.600
what was promised.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.480
That means fewer messages asking for
answers, fewer calls to check progress,

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.040
and less chasing people to get things
done. When people do need to talk,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.880
the agents turn that conversation into
work in real time. Imagine a week

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.880
with 30 fewer interruptions

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.690
and zero follow-ups to remember. Your team
gets what they need, you get the

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.360
time and the headspace

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.410
to focus on where you can make the biggest
impact. Slack's mission is to

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.120
make work life simpler,

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.240
more pleasant, and more productive. That
means fewer interruptions to get an
answer,

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.060
fewer follow-ups to make a decision, and
less time catching up before you

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.120
can contribute.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.280
People will still come together for the
questions that need their judgment,

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:47.040
and while they talk, the producer will
turn their decisions into work.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.760
And afterwards, their twins will update
the people who need to know.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.400
That's how we make most conversations
unnecessary.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.000
By taking care of the questions and
coordinations that call to them.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.940
Imagine starting Monday ready to
contribute because catching up is already

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:03.840
taken care of.
